Think of regex like a mini-language for pattern matching.
Each part has a meaning:

^ → Start of the string
[a-zA-Z0-9_.+-]+ → Username part:
Allowed characters:
a-z → lowercase letters
A-Z → uppercase letters
0-9 → digits
_ . + - → underscore, dot, plus, minus
+ → means "one or more" of these characters
@ → The literal @ symbol
[a-zA-Z0-9-]+ → Domain name (letters, digits, or -)
\. → A dot . (escaped, since . normally means "any character")
[a-zA-Z0-9-.]+ → Top-level domain (e.g., .com, .org, .co.in)
$ → End of the string
🔹 Why call it a "model"?
Because it models the shape of an email address.
It doesn’t store data, but it describes the rules for what counts as valid.
For example:
✅ Matches:
abc@gmail.com
user.name+tag@sub-domain.org
X123@domain.co.in
❌ Does not match:
plainaddress
@no-username.com
user@.com
